A Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 Ss. 166, 163A and 168Just compensation Law clarified on the following aspects:
(A) Deduction of personal and living expenses to determine multiplicand;
(B) Selection of multiplier depending on age of deceased;
Triveni.S vs Icici Lomb Gen Ins Co Ltd on 14 June, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/169877118/
4 (C) Age of deceased as basis for applying multiplier;
(D) Reasonable figures on conventional heads, namely, loss of estate, loss of consortium and Funeral expenses, with escalation;
(E) Future prospects for all categories of persons and for different ages: with permanent job; self employed or fixed salary 1) On deduction towards personal and living expenses in paras 30, 31 and 32, Sarla Vema, (2009)6 SCC.121 lays down: (SCC p. 136) AIR 2009 SUPREME COURT 3104 Where the deceased was married, the deduction towards personal and living expenses of the deceased, should be one third (1/3rd) where the number of dependent family members is 2 to 3, onefourth (1/4th) where the number of dependent family members is 4 to 6, and onefifth (1/5th) where the number of dependent family members exceeds six.
where the deceased was a bachelor and the claimants are the parents, the deduction follows a different principle.
